rd; lowercase human lord and sentence-title Lord are not inferred from this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord","term":"LORD"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Lord","sourceTitle":"Lord"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Pr.5.1-23","r":"Pr.5.1-23","rawR":"Pr.5.1-23","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.5.1-23","value":"Pr.5.1-23"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:1-23"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," These instructions on embracing one’s wife and avoiding immoral women were originally addressed to young men just beginning their professional careers (see also ",{"href":"?bref=Pr.2.16-22","r":"Pr.2.16-22","rawR":"Pr.2.16-22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.2.16-22","value":"Pr.2.16-22"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:16-22"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Pr.7.1-27","r":"Pr.7.1-27","rawR":"Pr.7.1-27","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.7.1-27","value":"Pr.7.1-27"}],"t":"ref","v":"7:1-27"},"). This topic is also popular in ancient Egyptian wisdom writing."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Pr.5.1-23","refs":[{"book":"PRO","endChapter":5,"endVerse":23,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Pr.5.1-23","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"PRO","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"8b9bbe118abb343a42b8591ac1ed6586e5b51d323787bf936ccf12cf32a3cfdf","sourceItemName":"Pr.5.1-23","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7546,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"PRO","canonicalOSIS":"Prov","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Pr"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"resolved_source_book_alias","sourceRefsExact":"Pr.5.1-23","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Pr.5.1-23","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Pr.5.1-2","r":"Pr.5.1-2","rawR":"Pr.5.1-2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.5.1-2","value":"Pr.5.1-2"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:1-2"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Paying ",{"c":["attention"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," to instruction results in ",{"c":["discernment"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},"—the ability to tell a right action from a wrong one. These instructions are vitally important when dealing with an immoral woman."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Pr.5.1-2","refs":[{"book":"PRO","endChapter":5,"endVerse":2,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Pr.5.1-2","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"PRO","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"a11c26b3bf85eb9de5a6dde051f262ad69c6d3fb7b4377fed862e8ec153cc115","sourceItemName":"Pr.5.1-2","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7547,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"PRO","canonicalOSIS":"Prov","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Pr"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"resolved_source_book_alias","sourceRefsExact":"Pr.5.1-2","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Pr.5.1-2","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Pr.5.3-4","r":"Pr.5.3-4","rawR":"Pr.5.3-4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.5.3-4","value":"Pr.5.3-4"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:3-4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The ",{"c":["lips"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," of the immoral woman seem sweet and enticing but are actually bitter and deadly. Sexual activity often begins with a kiss, and flattering words can entice."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Pr.5.3-4","refs":[{"book":"PRO","endChapter":5,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":3}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Pr.5.3-4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"PRO","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"3fde69541c6a64ac0eef83c5c39424f1f6baa4fd35e5fd0dd52572209a22d818","sourceItemName":"Pr.5.3-4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7548,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"PRO","canonicalOSIS":"Prov","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Pr"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"resolved_source_book_alias","sourceRefsExact":"Pr.5.3-4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Pr.5.3-4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Pr.5.5-6","r":"Pr.5.5-6","rawR":"Pr.5.5-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.5.5-6","value":"Pr.5.5-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:5-6"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," A relationship with an immoral woman leads ",{"c":["to death,"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," as does a relationship with the woman named Folly (see ",{"href":"?bref=Pr.9.13-18","r":"Pr.9.13-18","rawR":"Pr.9.13-18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.9.13-18","value":"Pr.9.13-18"}],"t":"ref","v":"9:13-18"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Pr.5.5-6","refs":[{"book":"PRO","endChapter":5,"endVerse":6,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":5}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Pr.5.5-6","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"PRO","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"6e6b60a699c71f9e7e46031b9414b5ad9c84184dc937672ef3475cc39a918eea","sourceItemName":"Pr.5.5-6","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7549,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"PRO","canonicalOSIS":"Prov","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Pr"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"resolved_source_book_alias","sourceRefsExact":"Pr.5.5-6","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Pr.5.5-6","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Pr.5.9-10","r":"Pr.5.9-10","rawR":"Pr.5.9-10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.5.9-10","value":"Pr.5.9-10"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:9-10"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," An illicit relationship can be tempting, but it eventually brings shame. If the woman is a prostitute, the young man will have to pay her and thus lose income. If the woman is another man’s wife, he might have to pay her husband (see ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.20.10","r":"Lev.20.10","rawR":"Lev.20.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.20.10","value":"Lev.20.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lev 20:10"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Pr.5.9-10","refs":[{"book":"PRO","endChapter":5,"endVerse":10,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":9}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Pr.5.9-10","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"PRO","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"f087af77b3c3c31ab5917585e2bb8814b53b24972d642cf98dbab270553fcd17","sourceItemName":"Pr.5.9-10","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7550,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"PRO","canonicalOSIS":"Prov","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Pr"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"resolved_source_book_alias","sourceRefsExact":"Pr.5.9-10","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Pr.5.9-10","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Pr.5.11","r":"Pr.5.11","rawR":"Pr.5.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.5.11","value":"Pr.5.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:11"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Some sexually transmitted diseases were well known in the ancient Near East. ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.15.1-15","r":"Lev.15.1-15","rawR":"Lev.15.1-15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.15.1-15","value":"Lev.15.1-15"}],"t":"ref","v":"Leviticus 15:1-15"}," describes what is probably gonorrhea; other texts from Mesopotamia describe “the disease of intercourse” and the “disease of Ishtar” (goddess of love) with the physical symptoms of venereal diseases."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Pr.5.11","refs":[{"book":"PRO","endChapter":5,"endVerse":11,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":11}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Pr.5.11","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"PRO","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"050f9b8aa22799505414d19cf6799b4132fdc4d4c3f9c0633e8cd6e5f0430af8","sourceItemName":"Pr.5.11","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7551,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"PRO","canonicalOSIS":"Prov","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Pr"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"resolved_source_book_alias","sourceRefsExact":"Pr.5.11","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Pr.5.11","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Pr.5.15-18","r":"Pr.5.15-18","rawR":"Pr.5.15-18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.5.15-18","value":"Pr.5.15-18"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:15-18"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," To ",{"c":["drink"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," from one’s own ",{"c":["well"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (see also ",{"href":"?bref=Song.4.12","r":"Song.4.12","rawR":"Song.4.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.4.12","value":"Song.4.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Song 4:12"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Song.4.15","r":"Song.4.15","rawR":"Song.4.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.4.15","value":"Song.4.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"15"},") is to enjoy sexual relations within marriage. Rather than expend sexual energy on immoral women, a man should cultivate a healthy sexual relationship with his wife. Sex is not to be shared with ",{"c":["strangers"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},". This honors marriage (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.2.22-25","r":"Gen.2.22-25","rawR":"Gen.2.22-25","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.2.22-25","value":"Gen.2.22-25"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gen 2:22-25"},") and keeps the seventh commandment (",{"href":"?bref=Exod.20.14","r":"Exod.20.14","rawR":"Exod.20.14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.20.14","value":"Exod.20.14"}],"t":"ref","v":"Exod 20:14"}," and ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.5.18","r":"Deut.5.18","rawR":"Deut.5.18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.5.18","value":"Deut.5.18"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 5:18"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Pr.5.15-18","refs":[{"book":"PRO","endChapter":5,"endVerse":18,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":15}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Pr.5.15-18","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"PRO","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"4ff23e41acddb664fd4f32a637034e328e4c63b3e65cfb7a5cf8d65d953c57a2","sourceItemName":"Pr.5.15-18","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7552,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"PRO","canonicalOSIS":"Prov","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Pr"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"resolved_source_book_alias","sourceRefsExact":"Pr.5.15-18","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Pr.5.15-18","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Pr.5.19","r":"Pr.5.19","rawR":"Pr.5.19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.5.19","value":"Pr.5.19"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:19"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["deer:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Song.4.5","r":"Song.4.5","rawR":"Song.4.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.4.5","value":"Song.4.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"Song 4:5"},"."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Pr.5.19","refs":[{"book":"PRO","endChapter":5,"endVerse":19,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":19}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Pr.5.19","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"PRO","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"0c4f86d2dcf4835471969eeb271c5d2e119bf2514caedbcc0a34b292be07beb2","sourceItemName":"Pr.5.19","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7553,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"PRO","canonicalOSIS":"Prov","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Pr"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"resolved_source_book_alias","sourceRefsExact":"Pr.5.19","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Pr.5.19","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Pr.5.21-23","r":"Pr.5.21-23","rawR":"Pr.5.21-23","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.5.21-23","value":"Pr.5.21-23"}],"t":"ref","v":"5:21-23"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Even if a man keeps an illicit relationship secret from his family and society, nothing is hidden from ",{"c":["the"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Lord"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt-divine-name","t":"span"},". Adultery is a sin against God (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.39.8-9","r":"Gen.39.8-9","rawR":"Gen.39.8-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.39.8-9","value":"Gen.39.8-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gen 39:8-9"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Pr.5.21-23","refs":[{"book":"PRO","endChapter":5,"endVerse":23,"startChapter":5,"startVerse":21}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Pr.5.21-23","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"PRO","sourceChapterCoverage":[5],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"753114676919505e435e0a086d5f38599ff82f03165ce4b8b80828ed8c755b64","sourceItemName":"Pr.5.21-23","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xm